WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:05.880
By the next morning, November 18, I had recovered from our adventures of the previous day.

2
00:00:05.881 --> 00:00:12.221
I went up on the platform, where Captain Nemo soon appeared and began a series of observations with his instruments.

3
00:00:12.222 --> 00:00:17.380
Meanwhile some sailors came up to haul in the fishing nets they'd laid the previous night.

4
00:00:17.381 --> 00:00:24.713
Although they appeared to be Europeans, they all used that strange language, so I couldn't determine their nationalities.

5
00:00:24.714 --> 00:00:30.784
Nemo suddenly began speaking to me. "Professor, isn't this ocean like a living being?

6
00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:37.451
It has its moods. Last night it slept and now it has awoken after a quiet night."

7
00:00:37.452 --> 00:00:41.594
When Captain Nemo spoke like this, he seemed like a different man.

8
00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:50.761
"I can imagine," he went on, "clusters of submarine houses that would rise every morning to breathe like the Nautilus

9
00:00:50.762 --> 00:00:57.007
. Free towns, independent cities. Yet who knows whether some

10
00:00:57.008 --> 00:01:03.919
tyrant—" Captain Nemo interrupted himself with a violent gesture. Our conversation was over without my having said a

11
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:10.665
word. During the weeks that followed, I seldom saw him, and when I did, he felt no need to explain his

12
00:01:10.666 --> 00:01:16.570
absence. His lieutenant regularly marked the submarine's course on the map, so I always knew where we

13
00:01:16.571 --> 00:01:23.411
were. By November 27 we had traveled 4,860 leagues and reached

14
00:01:23.412 --> 00:01:31.721
Hawaii. On December 1 we crossed the equator. From December 4 to 11, we traveled an additional 925

15
00:01:31.722 --> 00:01:37.149
leagues. We passed December 25 without celebrating Christmas, much to Ned's

16
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:43.713
regret. Early on the morning of January 1, 1868, Conseil joined me on the

17
00:01:43.714 --> 00:01:47.974
platform. "Master, may I wish you a Happy New Year?" he

18
00:01:47.975 --> 00:01:55.784
asked. "I thank you for your good wishes," I said. "But I must ask what you mean by a 'happy' year in this

19
00:01:55.785 --> 00:02:03.212
situation. Do you mean a year that will bring us to the end of our imprisonment or a year that sees us continue on this strange voyage?"

20
00:02:03.213 --> 00:02:10.450
"I don't really know how to answer, Master. We're sure to see some interesting things if we continue with Captain Nemo.

21
00:02:10.451 --> 00:02:17.117
In the past two months we haven't had time to be bored; the latest marvel is always the most astonishing.

22
00:02:17.118 --> 00:02:21.109
So I'd say a happy year would be one where we could see everything—"

23
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:27.847
"Everything, Conseil? No year could be that long. But what does Ned Land think about all this?"

24
00:02:27.848 --> 00:02:32.910
"Ned's thoughts are exactly the opposite of mine," said Conseil.

25
00:02:32.911 --> 00:02:38.434
"He's tired of staring at fish through the windows and eating them at every meal."

26
00:02:38.435 --> 00:02:41.172
"Oh, I don't mind the food," I said.

27
00:02:41.173 --> 00:02:47.593
"Neither do I," replied Conseil. "I think as much about staying as Ned does about escaping.

28
00:02:47.594 --> 00:02:51.751
So no matter what happens, one of us will be happy.

29
00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:56.323
In conclusion I wish for you to have whatever will make you happy."

30
00:02:56.324 --> 00:03:00.148
We shook hands and Conseil descended into the submarine.

31
00:03:00.149 --> 00:03:06.752
By the next day, January 2, we had traveled 5,250 leagues under the sea.

32
00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:14.236
Over the next two days, we crossed the Coral Sea, and on January 4, we sighted the coast of New Guinea.

33
00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:23.133
On that day Captain Nemo announced that he intended to get to the Indian Ocean by the Torres Strait between New Guinea and Australia.

34
00:03:23.134 --> 00:03:31.950
Filled with coral reefs, it was a route that even the boldest navigators feared, and many considered the world's most dangerous strait.

35
00:03:31.951 --> 00:03:39.458
The passage is about seventy miles wide, but it's obstructed by countless islands, islets, and rocks.

36
00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:45.204
It's almost impossible to navigate, so Captain Nemo had to take every precaution.

37
00:03:45.205 --> 00:03:49.855
Rising to the surface, the Nautilus proceeded slowly through the water.

38
00:03:49.856 --> 00:03:55.855
My two companions and I took advantage of the situation and climbed to the deserted platform.

39
00:03:55.856 --> 00:04:04.601
I suspected that Captain Nemo was piloting the sub himself. Around the Nautilus the sea appeared to boil as waves broke over the coral.

40
00:04:04.602 --> 00:04:09.760
"This is a rough sea," said Ned. "I hope the captain knows what he's doing."

41
00:04:09.761 --> 00:04:16.760
Indeed the situation was dangerous, but the Nautilus seemed to glide through the water like magic.

42
00:04:16.761 --> 00:04:22.585
At about three in the afternoon, we were only a couple miles away from the island of Gilboa.

43
00:04:22.586 --> 00:04:26.585
Suddenly I was knocked off my feet—we had run aground!

44
00:04:26.586 --> 00:04:33.323
The Nautilus had just touched the coral below, but the vessel was now listing slightly to its portside.

45
00:04:33.324 --> 00:04:38.141
When I stood up, I saw that Captain Nemo and his lieutenant were on the platform.

46
00:04:38.142 --> 00:04:43.236
They were examining the situation and talking in their incomprehensible language.

47
00:04:43.237 --> 00:04:48.982
Unfortunately we had run aground in a part of the ocean where the tides were not strong.

48
00:04:48.983 --> 00:04:53.808
It would be difficult for the tide to rise high enough to float the sub off the coral.

49
00:04:53.809 --> 00:04:59.300
I was thinking about this when the captain, cool and calm as ever, approached me.

50
00:04:59.301 --> 00:05:01.535
"An accident?" I asked.

51
00:05:01.536 --> 00:05:04.705
"No, an incident," he replied.

52
00:05:04.706 --> 00:05:10.213
"But an incident that may oblige you to become an inhabitant of land once more?"

53
00:05:10.214 --> 00:05:16.133
Captain Nemo shook his head as if to say that nothing could force him to set foot on dry land again.

54
00:05:16.134 --> 00:05:23.959
"The Nautilus is not damaged, Professor. It will still carry you into the midst of the ocean's marvels," he replied.

55
00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:31.031
"Our voyage has only begun, and I would not want to be deprived so soon of the honor of your company."

56
00:05:31.032 --> 00:05:34.180
"But the tides aren't very high here," I said.

57
00:05:34.181 --> 00:05:40.774
"That is true, but in five days, the moon will be full, so the tide will be at its highest. I

58
00:05:40.775 --> 00:05:44.766
fully expect the moon to take care of this incident for me." Having

59
00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:50.206
said that, Captain Nemo and his lieutenant went back into the interior of the submarine. Once

60
00:05:50.207 --> 00:05:54.348
they were gone, Ned and Conseil approached me. "Well,

61
00:05:54.349 --> 00:05:59.261
sir?" asked Ned. "What did Captain Nemo have to say?" I

62
00:05:59.262 --> 00:06:03.936
explained how the captain said we must wait for the full moon to do its work. "Really?"

63
00:06:03.937 --> 00:06:09.102
said Ned skeptically. "We're just going to sit here?" "The

64
00:06:09.103 --> 00:06:14.570
captain is not going to do anything to try and move the Nautilus ?" asked Conseil.

65
00:06:14.571 --> 00:06:16.999
"That is correct," I said.

66
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.316
Ned looked at Conseil and then shrugged his shoulders.

67
00:06:20.317 --> 00:06:25.745
"Sir, believe me, this hunk of metal is never going to navigate the sea again.

68
00:06:25.746 --> 00:06:31.642
It's only fit to be sold for scrap, therefore, I think it's time to part company with Captain Nemo."

69
00:06:31.643 --> 00:06:36.563
I still had faith in the captain and his vessel, so I counseled Ned to stay put.

70
00:06:36.564 --> 00:06:42.904
"Flight might be possible if we were in sight of the English or French coasts, but out here—"

71
00:06:42.905 --> 00:06:46.301
"Couldn't we at least go ashore?" asked Ned.

72
00:06:46.302 --> 00:06:52.713
"There are likely to be animals roaming the island, and I'm sure they could be turned into fine roasts and cutlets."

73
00:06:52.714 --> 00:06:56.483
"Master, I agree with Ned," said Conseil.

74
00:06:56.484 --> 00:07:04.221
"Couldn't you ask your friend, Captain Nemo, to put us ashore, if only so we don't forget how to walk on land?"

75
00:07:04.222 --> 00:07:07.459
"I'll ask, but I know he'll refuse."

76
00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:11.443
To my great surprise, the captain agreed to our request.

77
00:07:11.444 --> 00:07:18.189
He didn't even make us promise to return, probably knowing that fleeing across New Guinea would be perilous.

78
00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:28.135
I'd decided that if Ned asked me about escaping, I'd say it was better to be a prisoner onboard the Nautilus than to fall into the hands of the natives.

